    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was a duchy from c. 555 to 1623, ruled in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfings</a>, Frankish kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luitpoldings">Luitpoldings</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a>.</li><li>Bavarian settlers and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and German speech into the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</li><li>Under Maximilian I it led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> and in 1623 was raised to an electorate, later the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> began as an eastern frontier march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and only became a separate duchy in 1156; Bavarian dialects are still spoken across most of Austria.</p></aside>
    </section>

