    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodelinda.jpg" alt="Theodelinda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodelinda_married_Agilulf_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fratelli Zavattari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, or <em>Baiuvarii</em>, first appear in written sources in the mid 6th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> mentions them east of the Swabians. They were not a single migrating tribe but a people formed in the former Roman provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> and Noricum, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-river">Lech</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, from Germanic groups and the Romanised population that had remained. Around 555 the Frankish kings recognised Garibald I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfing</a> family as duke of the region, and he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walderada">Walderada</a>, widow of the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theudebald-duke-of-alamannia">Theudebald</a>. From the start the duchy stood under Frankish overlordship, yet the Agilolfings had wide connections: Garibald's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a> married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a> in 589 and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a>, and became a leading figure in Lombard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>The duchy was refounded several times. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> deposed the last Agilolfing in 788 it was governed by Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> and then became the core of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>. In the early 10th century, amid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids, the Luitpolding Arnulf made it a stem duchy within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, and it remained one of the great duchies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/passau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/passau.jpg" alt="Passau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PassauCollection2025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Compiled by Viceskeeni2, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was an agrarian land of grain-growing valleys, pasture and forest, with Roman towns such as Regensburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castra-regina">Castra Regina</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> surviving as church and trading centres. Salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-reichenhall">Reichenhall</a> was a valuable export, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> carried trade towards the east; the Stone Bridge at Regensburg, built in the 1130s and 1140s, served one of the busiest commercial cities of the Empire. Christianisation was carried out by missionaries such as Rupert at Salzburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emmeram">Emmeram</a> at Regensburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corbinian">Corbinian</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> around 700, and in 739 Boniface organised the bishoprics of Salzburg, Regensburg, Freising and Passau. Ducal monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegernsee-abbey">Tegernsee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kremsmunster-abbey">Kremsmünster</a> became among the largest landowners in the duchy. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in 1158, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> grew under ducal protection. The dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> were so wealthy that they were known as 'the Rich Dukes'. Society remained strongly Catholic: the dukes rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> to Ingolstadt in the 1550s and made Bavaria a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. In 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-duke-of-bavaria">William IV</a> and Louis X issued an ordinance on beer that is often cited as the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinheitsgebot">Reinheitsgebot</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg.jpg" alt="Battle of Pressburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg">Battle of Pressburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Pressburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peter Johann Nepomuk Geiger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfings</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s forces fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> on its eastern and southern frontiers, and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III</a> subdued Carantania, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, around 772. Attempts to resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> failed: Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odilo-of-bavaria">Odilo</a> was defeated by Pippin and Carloman on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-river">Lech</a> in 743. In the 9th century Bavaria was the base for Frankish campaigns against the Avars, Moravians and Slavs. A Bavarian army under Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luitpold-prince-regent-of-bavaria">Luitpold</a> was annihilated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg">Battle of Pressburg</a> in 907, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-duke-of-bavaria">Arnulf the Bad</a> defended the duchy by negotiating with and fighting the raiders. Bavarian contingents fought in the decisive victory of Otto I over the Magyars at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 955. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> the duchy's wars were often internal: partitions led to fraternal conflicts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">Landshut War of Succession</a> of 1503–1505 ended only through imperial arbitration. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a>, elected king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, defeated his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhldorf">Battle of Mühldorf</a> in 1322. Maximilian I built a disciplined standing force, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> in 1609 and, with its general Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Tilly</a>, shared in the Catholic victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1620.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum.jpg" alt="Lex Baiuvariorum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum">Lex Baiuvariorum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prolog_lex_baioariorum_ingolstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Schreiber unbekannt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfing</a> dukes ruled almost as kings. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum">Lex Baiuvariorum</a></em>, a law code compiled probably in the 740s under Frankish influence, named the Agilolfings as the hereditary ducal family and protected five other noble kindreds. The dukes held assemblies, founded monasteries and presided over synods of their own church. After 788 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was governed by Frankish officials and the Carolingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, who resided at Regensburg. As a stem duchy of the Empire it passed between families: the Ottonian kings gave it to relatives, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a> held it from 1070 with interruptions, and in 1180 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> and granted the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto of Wittelsbach</a>, whose family ruled Bavaria until 1918.</p><p>Wittelsbach rule alternated between unity and division. The duchy was partitioned in 1255 into Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>, reunited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a> in 1340, and divided again in 1349 and 1392 into lines based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a>. The estates of nobles, clergy and towns gained privileges such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-handfeste">Ottonian Handfeste</a> of 1311 and met in diets. Albert IV reunited the country after 1505 and decreed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> in 1506, and his successors built a centralised administration with a privy council and court chamber in Munich.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied.jpg" alt="Nibelungenlied" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nibelungenlied_manuscript-c_f1r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s influence was greatest along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Bavarian settlers and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, raised to an archbishopric in 798, carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and German speech into Carantania and the lands that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which began as a Bavarian frontier march under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a> in 976 and was made a separate duchy only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-minus">Privilegium Minus</a> of 1156. The Bavarian dialects spoken today in Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> reflect this expansion. Bavarian scriptoria preserved important early texts, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of around 1000, the oldest surviving writing in a Slavic language in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> was probably composed in the Danube region around 1200. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a>, the first Wittelsbach emperor, opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and sheltered critics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilius-of-padua">Marsilius of Padua</a> at his court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. In the 16th century the Munich court of Albert V employed the composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orlande-de-lassus">Orlande de Lassus</a> and built collections that grew into the Bavarian State Library. By championing Catholic reform and leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>, the dukes made Bavaria the principal Catholic power of the Empire after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, a role that shaped the course of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> did not decline so much as change its form. Its lowest points came in 788, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III</a>, and in the 10th to 12th centuries, when the kings repeatedly detached territory: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-verona">March of Verona</a> were split off in 976, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1156 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>'s link was broken in 1180. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> repeated partitions weakened the duchy for two and a half centuries, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">Landshut War of Succession</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> arbitration of 1505, which reunited most of the lands under Albert IV, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> was carved out for his rivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">Primogeniture</a> from 1506 prevented further division.</p><p>Duke Maximilian I (1597–1651) turned Bavaria into a well-organised Catholic state. In 1607 he executed an imperial ban against the Protestant town of Donauwörth and annexed it, and after the Battle of White Mountain Emperor Ferdinand II rewarded him at Regensburg in 1623 with the electoral dignity taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V of the Palatinate</a>. With this the duchy became the Electorate of Bavaria, which gained the Upper Palatinate in 1628 and in 1806 became the Kingdom of Bavaria, the forerunner of the present German state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>For more than a thousand years the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was one of the great territorial units of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and German speech into the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, giving rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> becoming the leading Catholic power of the Empire before its elevation to an electorate in 1623.</em></p>

